 "guide": {
  "kazanim": [
   "TAOB.1 Choosing visual materials such as picture story books: Looks closely at the cover pictures one by one (TAOB.1.a) and chooses the book they want to read from the options (TAOB.1.b).",
   "TADB.1 Managing the materials they will listen to or watch: Chooses what they want to listen to from the books on offer (TADB.1.a).",
   "OB1 Information Literacy and OB4 Visual Literacy: Says what the cover picture shows (bear, car, star) and puts the book on the shelf whose picture belongs to the same group (bear → the animal shelf with the cat).",
   "SDB3.3 Responsible Decision-Making and D11 Freedom: When muddled, stops, breathes, compares the options and gives a reason for their choice (“Because the star is smiling at me”); respects a friend's different choice."
  ],
  "once": [
   "Look at the cover: “Where is Can? What do you think he'll do today?” Listen to the guesses without correcting them.",
   "Look at the classroom book corner together: “How do we choose a book here?”",
   "Ask “What do you do when you feel muddled?”; take one deep breath in and out together."
  ],
  "sirasi": [
   "On page 2, let the child find the hidden books; repeat the position words together: “under the table, behind the cushion, next to the box”.",
   "On page 3, breathe in through the nose with Can (Hhhhh…) and blow out through the mouth (Fwooo!). For the prediction question, say that both options are good ideas.",
   "On page 4, the child touches a book first, then the shelf with a picture from the same group (a bear and a cat are both animals). Ask “How did you work it out?”; talk about the star–sun pair together (both are in the sky).",
   "On page 5, accept every answer to “Which one would you choose?”; asking for a reason with “because…” is enough."
  ],
  "sonra": [
   "Why did Can feel muddled? What did he do to feel better?",
   "What does the picture on a cover tell us? What might the bear book be about?",
   "Can chose the star book, Deniz chose the bear book. Are both right? Why?"
  ],
  "sinif": {
   "ad": "Picture shelf labels and cover detectives",
   "sure": "20 min",
   "materyal": "9–12 picture books from the class library; three picture shelf cards (a cat for animals, a bus for vehicles, a sun for the sky); tape; a reading cushion",
   "adim": [
    "Tape the three picture cards to three shelves of the bookcase; talk with the children about what each card means.",
    "Spread the books on the carpet. Each child takes a book, looks closely at the cover and says “This book has … in it, because I saw … on the cover.”",
    "The child puts the book on the shelf whose picture belongs to the same group as the cover (the bear book on the animal shelf with the cat); discuss undecided books as a class (a book can fit two shelves!).",
    "Finally, each child chooses a book from the shelf, says in one sentence why they chose it and looks at it with a friend on the reading cushion."
   ]
  },
  "evSoru": [
   "Which of your books at home is your favourite? What's on the cover?",
   "How can you guess what a book is about from its cover?",
   "Was there a time when choosing was hard for you? What did you do?"
  ],
  "evEtk": {
   "ad": "You choose tonight's book",
   "d": "Every evening, offer your child two or three books and let them choose the one to read. Look at the covers together first: “What do you see here? What do you think it's about?” When they've chosen, ask “Why did you choose this one?” and accept their answer. At the weekend you can sort the books at home onto little shelves with picture cards (animals, vehicles, sky)."
  },
  "aileNotu": "Today we read “Can Chooses His Book”. When a ball rolled away and the books tumbled down, Can couldn't decide; he took a deep breath, looked closely at the cover pictures and chose his own book, with a reason. The story supports visual reading (making meaning from a cover picture), choosing books and responsible decision-making. Letting your child choose the bedtime book at home and asking “Why did you choose this one?” strengthens these skills."
 },
